import {writeLesson,writeAssessment,section as s,choice as c,numeric as n,open as o,diagram as d,source} from './content-kit.mjs';
const rabbit=source('RabbitMQ documentation: consumer acknowledgements and publisher confirms','https://www.rabbitmq.com/docs/confirms');
const queues=source('RabbitMQ documentation: queue ordering and consumer behavior','https://www.rabbitmq.com/docs/queues');
const aws=source('Amazon Builders’ Library: making retries safe with idempotent APIs','https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/making-retries-safe-with-idempotent-APIs/');
const sql=source('Python sqlite3 documentation: transaction control','https://docs.python.org/3/library/sqlite3.html');
const pacing='Core55 minutes: retrieval5, mechanism18, worked flow8, exercise10, questions10, review4.';

writeLesson('091',{objectives:['Choose between work distribution, fanout and replayable history.','Identify who owns processing progress and message retention.','Explain why adding a queue changes timing rather than business correctness.'],retrieval:['L007: what happens when arrival exceeds completion capacity?','L027: why is an asynchronous response not the final business outcome?'],sections:[
s('needs','Three different reasons to send a message',`${pacing} A campus media service has three needs: one worker should resize each uploaded image; search and analytics should independently learn of new uploads; and a new recommendation experiment should replay last month’s events. All involve messages, but they ask for different ownership and retention semantics.

A work queue distributes units among competing consumers so one worker normally handles a delivery. A pub/sub arrangement fans information to multiple interested subscribers, whose independent progress may require separate durable subscriptions. An append-only log retains an ordered history for a chosen period, while each consumer group tracks a position. A technology can support more than one pattern; start with the needed behavior instead of assuming a product name determines it.

The image-resize job might be deleted or archived after successful completion. A retained event log can preserve upload facts even after one consumer finishes. A search consumer catching up must not imply that analytics has caught up. Ask who can replay, how long data is retained, and whether one slow consumer affects another.`),
s('ownership','Define the handoff rather than drawing an arrow',`The producer owns validating and durably recording the fact or requested work before it can responsibly report acceptance. The messaging system owns a documented delivery/retention contract. The consumer owns applying a business effect and recording its processing position according to that contract. These boundaries do not form one automatic transaction merely because the architecture diagram connects them.

Queue decoupling can let an API return accepted quickly while work finishes later. The product now needs a job/status identity and a way to distinguish accepted, running, succeeded and failed. If the user needs the resized image immediately, moving resize to a queue does not remove that requirement; it changes the interaction to polling, notification or a later fetch.

Ordering is scoped. One ordered partition or queue does not guarantee that multiple consumers finish in the same order, especially with retries. Independent partitions improve parallelism while weakening global order. Choose the key whose operations must be serialized, such as one account or conversation, and explain how hot keys limit that parallelism.`),
s('flow','Trace one upload through independent consumers',`An upload API validates ownership and stores a durable object reference. It records an upload event with event_id, object_id, actor_id and schema_version. A resize consumer creates a derived thumbnail; an indexing consumer updates search; an audit consumer retains a trace. Each should be able to retry without confusing the same logical event with a new one.

For a retained log, imagine partition0 has events at offsets101–110. The indexing group has processed through108 and analytics through104. Their lag is2 and6 events respectively if110 is the latest available offset and offsets are contiguous in this teaching example. Advancing indexing to109 must not advance analytics. Keeping a single shared cursor would couple the groups and lose one group’s work.

These are illustrative records, not executable broker configuration:

~~~python
event = {'event_id': 'up-17', 'object_id': 'obj-9', 'schema_version': 1}
positions = {'search': {0: 108}, 'analytics': {0: 104}}
~~~

The event identifies a fact; the group/partition cursor identifies processing progress. Replaying the fact can be useful, but the derived effect must tolerate replay if it should not duplicate.`),
s('limits','A buffer buys time, not capacity',`If arrivals sustain120 jobs/s while workers finish100 jobs/s, backlog grows by20/s. A queue of10,000 absorbs about500 seconds from empty under that constant-rate model. It does not make the workload stable. The system needs greater service capacity, lower admitted demand, a changed service target or explicit rejection/degradation.

Measure arrival/completion rates, oldest-message age, per-group lag, failure rate and retry volume. Queue depth alone can look modest while a few old messages violate the user’s deadline. Retention can expire work before a slow consumer catches up; that is a product failure mode to define, not a detail to discover after loss.

Common misconceptions include treating pub/sub as guaranteed durable replay, equating dequeue order with completion order, and assuming asynchronous delivery makes a write atomic with its downstream effects. L092 and L093 examine those boundaries directly.`)
],diagrams:[d('Independent consumers own independent progress','flowchart LR\nP[Upload fact] --> L[Retained partitioned log]\nL -->|search group cursor| S[Search projection]\nL -->|analytics group cursor| A[Analytics projection]\nL -->|audit group cursor| U[Audit sink]','Each group advances its own position. The log’s retention policy controls how far a disconnected group can replay.',['Persist an identifiable fact.','Deliver independently to interested groups.','Record each group’s progress separately.','Rebuild derived state within retention constraints.'])],exercise:{prompt:'Choose a pattern for email-send jobs, independent billing/audit reactions, and rebuilding a search projection from historical events. State one ordering key and one retention/retry question for each.',rubric:['Matches processing ownership to pattern.','Separates fanout groups.','Names retention and duplicate-effect limits.'],solution:'Email work can use competing consumers with explicit acknowledgement and retry/dead-letter policy; an account or campaign key may matter if send order is required. Billing and audit need separate durable consumers/subscriptions so one’s completion does not discard the other’s work. Search rebuild benefits from retained replayable events with a documented retention horizon and per-group offsets, often partitioned by entity key. None of these choices alone makes external email or billing effects exactly once; the receiver needs a matching idempotency/effect protocol.'},questions:[c('q1','Two independent subscribers should normally share one processing cursor:',['Yes','No'],'No','Each owns its own progress; one subscriber finishing must not discard another’s work.'),n('q2','At120 arrivals/s and100 completions/s, backlog growth?',20,'jobs/s','120−100=20 jobs/s.'),c('q3','A retained log primarily separates:',['History retention from individual consumer progress','All failure handling from application code'],'History retention from individual consumer progress','Consumers track positions independently of the retained history.'),o('q4','Why does moving work to a queue change the API contract?','Acceptance can precede business completion, so the client needs an explicit status/result interaction and failure policy.')],flashcards:[['Work queue?','Competing consumers process units with a delivery/acknowledgement contract.'],['Pub/sub?','Independent subscribers receive related information.'],['Retained log?','History persists for a policy while groups track positions.'],['Ordered delivery implies ordered completion?','No; concurrency and retries can reorder completion.'],['Queue capacity creates throughput?','No; buffering absorbs temporary mismatch but does not remove sustained overload.']],mentalModel:'Choose who owns each unit of progress and how long the underlying fact remains replayable.',sources:[queues,rabbit]});

writeLesson('092',{objectives:['Separate publisher confirms from consumer acknowledgements.','Place acknowledgement relative to an effect and predict loss or duplicates.','Interpret delivery guarantees at an explicit boundary.'],retrieval:['L091: which actor owns a message after each handoff?','L083: why is a response lost after commit an uncertain outcome?'],sections:[
s('acks','Every acknowledgement answers a specific question',`${pacing} A producer publishes a job to a broker; the broker delivers it to a worker; the worker changes a database; the worker acknowledges completion. There are at least two different acknowledgement paths. A publisher confirm tells the producer something about the broker’s acceptance under the configured queue/durability semantics. A consumer acknowledgement tells the broker that a delivery can be considered handled. Neither automatically proves an external business effect happened correctly.

In RabbitMQ these are explicitly separate mechanisms. A confirm is not the consumer’s success result; a consumer acknowledgement is not a distributed transaction with the consumer’s database. Document the configured durability and replication behavior before translating a broker response into a business promise. A message accepted into a transient queue and one persisted under a replicated durable contract are not interchangeable evidence.

Delivery identifiers may be scoped to a connection or channel, while a business event ID persists across redelivery. Use the former correctly for the broker API and the latter for application identity; they solve different problems.`),
s('window','The order exposes the failure window',`If the worker acknowledges before applying its effect, a crash between ack and effect can lose the work: the broker believes delivery is complete and may not resend. This is compatible with an at-most-once style tradeoff but fails a requirement that every accepted job eventually has an effect.

If the worker applies the effect before acknowledging, a crash after effect but before ack can cause redelivery. The broker cannot know whether the effect committed. Reprocessing may execute it again unless the receiver deduplicates the logical operation. This is the common at-least-once tradeoff: duplicates are possible even when the broker and worker are behaving according to their contracts.

Neither ordering magically removes both windows across independent systems. The solution often combines at-least-once delivery with a persistent idempotent effect boundary, or uses a transactional protocol when all involved systems support the required participation. State the scope rather than saying “exactly once” without an object: exactly once delivery, handler invocation, stored row, or payment are different claims.`),
s('trace','A duplicate can be the safe recovery action',`Job J asks a worker to add ten loyalty points. The worker commits points+10 at time1 and crashes at time2 before acknowledging. The broker later redelivers J. If a new worker repeats points+10, the account gains twenty. A persistent inbox row keyed by J, committed in the same database transaction as the points update, can cause the retry to return the existing outcome without applying a second increment.

If the effect is an external email API, an inbox in the worker’s own database is not enough by itself. Marking the inbox before the email risks omission; marking it after risks duplicate email. The receiver’s idempotency feature, an explicitly tolerated duplicate, or another justified effect protocol is needed. L093 develops this boundary.

For broker handling, acknowledge only after the durable application boundary chosen for the contract. An example pseudocode trace is:

~~~python
delivery = receive()
result = apply_once_in_database(delivery.event_id, delivery.payload)
acknowledge(delivery.delivery_tag)
~~~

This is pseudocode: apply_once_in_database must actually include the marker and effect atomically; a function name is not evidence that it does.`),
s('retries','Redelivery must remain bounded and observable',`A poison payload or unavailable dependency can cause a tight reject/requeue loop. Immediate redelivery may consume CPU/network while completing no useful work. Use a retry classification, bounded attempts, backoff/delay and a dead-letter path with an operator workflow. Do not discard a valuable event silently after retries; record its identity and reason.

Prefetch or in-flight limits also matter. A worker allowed to hold thousands of unacknowledged deliveries may hoard work and increase recovery delay after a crash. A bounded number keeps resource consumption and redistribution more predictable. It does not guarantee fairness by itself.

Track confirmed publishes, unacknowledged deliveries, redeliveries, business effects and dead letters separately. A high acknowledgement rate can coexist with lost effects in an ack-before-commit bug. The useful question is always: what fact did this acknowledgement establish, at which authority?`)
],diagrams:[d('A crash after effect but before acknowledgement permits redelivery','sequenceDiagram\nparticipant B as Broker\nparticipant W as Worker\nparticipant D as Effect database\nB->>W: Delivery J\nW->>D: Commit effect for J\nD-->>W: Committed\nNote over W: Crash before acknowledgement\nB->>W: Redeliver J to replacement\nW->>D: Deduplicate J atomically\nW-->>B: Acknowledge delivery','The database must recognize J at the same atomic boundary as the effect. Broker redelivery is compatible with successful earlier processing.',['Deliver a stable business identity.','Commit the protected effect and identity together.','Treat lost ack as possible redelivery.','Return the stored outcome on retry.'])],exercise:{prompt:'Draw the two crash windows for ack-before-effect and effect-before-ack. Use an account-credit effect to propose a safe local database design, then identify why an external email receiver changes the argument.',rubric:['Identifies omission and duplication windows.','Uses one local transaction for marker/effect.','Does not extend local atomicity to external I/O.'],solution:'Ack-before-effect can omit the credit after a crash; effect-before-ack can repeat it after redelivery. Within one database, insert a unique inbox/event identity and apply the credit in the same transaction, rolling both back together on failure. A duplicate event returns the previously established result before the worker acknowledges its new delivery. Email is outside that transaction: a local marker cannot atomically establish whether an external send occurred. Require receiver idempotency, an explicit duplicate-tolerant contract or another supported protocol instead of claiming the same local pattern guarantees exactly one email.'},questions:[c('q1','A publisher confirm proves a consumer completed its business effect:',['Yes','No'],'No','Producer-to-broker confirmation and consumer-to-broker acknowledgement are separate boundaries.'),c('q2','Effect commit followed by a crash before ack can lead to:',['Redelivery','Proof that the effect failed'],'Redelivery','The broker lacks completion evidence and may resend an already-effected event.'),n('q3','A non-idempotent +10 operation runs twice after redelivery. Total increment?',20,'points','Two independent increments add20, demonstrating the duplicate-effect hazard.'),o('q4','Why can a local inbox fail to protect an external email send?','Its transaction cannot atomically include the independent receiver’s effect, leaving either a loss or duplicate window.')],flashcards:[['Publisher confirm?','Evidence about broker acceptance under its configured contract.'],['Consumer acknowledgement?','Tells broker that this delivery may be treated as handled.'],['Ack before effect risk?','Crash can lose work.'],['Effect before ack risk?','Crash can cause duplicate processing.'],['At-least-once plus idempotency?','Repeated deliveries can produce one protected logical effect at a defined boundary.']],mentalModel:'Acknowledge a fact you have actually made durable, and design for the uncertainty that remains between authorities.',sources:[rabbit]});

writeLesson('093',{objectives:['Scope an idempotency key to a logical request and actor.','Validate duplicate payload fingerprints and retain outcomes.','Place marker and effect inside one atomic boundary.'],retrieval:['L014: why is transport method idempotence different from a business-operation key?','L092: what happens if the effect commits before acknowledgement?'],sections:[
s('identity','Identify the logical operation, not the network attempt',`${pacing} A user clicks “reserve equipment,” the request times out, and the client retries. These are two network attempts for one intended reservation. A stable idempotency key lets the receiver recognize that identity. Generating a new key for every retry defeats the purpose; using one permanent key for every reservation prevents legitimate future requests.

Scope the key to the authenticated caller/tenant and operation family. A client-controlled key must not let one tenant retrieve another tenant’s saved result. Store a canonical fingerprint of the relevant request parameters. If a key already exists with a different fingerprint, return a conflict rather than reusing an unrelated response or applying a second mutation.

Fingerprinting is a contract decision: include fields that affect semantics, normalize only equivalences the API actually promises, and version the normalization if needed. A different JSON property order alone usually should not imply a different business request, but omitting a changed amount would be unsafe. Do not use a hash as authorization; it only summarizes the compared payload.`),
s('atomic','The unique key is necessary but not sufficient',`Within one SQL database, begin a transaction, establish a unique idempotency row scoped by tenant and key, perform the business mutation, and store the resulting response before commit. If the transaction fails, neither marker nor effect should remain partially applied. A duplicate sees the committed existing outcome; a competing insertion must follow the database’s conflict/wait semantics rather than racing through an application-only “if absent” check.

This SQL is a conceptual transaction pattern, not a complete portable handler:

~~~sql
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO request_results(tenant_id, request_key, fingerprint, status)
VALUES (:tenant, :key, :fingerprint, 'processing');
-- Apply the reservation using constraints/transaction checks.
UPDATE request_results SET status='done', response_json=:result
WHERE tenant_id=:tenant AND request_key=:key;
COMMIT;
~~~

A handler must distinguish first execution, an existing matching completed request, and a conflicting fingerprint. Long-running external work should not hold a database transaction open indefinitely; it needs a durable operation state with a recovery protocol. A permanent processing marker without takeover/timeout rules can strand work after a crash.`),
s('boundary','An inbox and an outbox answer different questions',`A consumer inbox records which incoming event identities have been applied. An outbox records outgoing work in the same transaction as the local business change so a separate publisher can retry delivery. An outbox publisher may publish more than once if it crashes after sending but before marking publication; downstream consumers still need duplicate tolerance. The outbox closes the local business-state versus publication-intent gap, not every downstream effect gap.

Worked flow: a reservation transaction stores reservation R and outbox event E together. A relay sends E, loses the response, then sends E again. A search consumer uses E as its inbox identity and updates its projection only once inside its own database transaction. Each authority owns a local atomic boundary, and the transport can remain at-least-once between them.

If E asks a payment provider to charge, the provider must accept the same logical idempotency key or offer another appropriate transaction/reconciliation mechanism. A local inbox marked before a provider call can suppress an effect that never occurred; marked afterward it can allow duplication. Name the effect receiver, not merely the local table.`),
s('retention','Identity retention is part of the guarantee',`Suppose the server discards request identities after24 hours, but a mobile client retries a queued operation three days later. The same key may look new and the business effect may repeat. Choose a supported retry horizon, retain keys/results long enough for it, and define behavior beyond the horizon. Some business identities are durable forever even if cached HTTP responses expire; distinguish those layers.

Bound stored payload/response sizes and avoid retaining unnecessary secrets. Track duplicate hits, fingerprint conflicts, stuck processing states and retries beyond policy. During recovery, reconcile uncertain external effects instead of choosing whichever state makes the queue empty fastest.

The central misconception is that a unique table row automatically establishes exactly-once end-to-end behavior. Its power comes from the transaction containing the protected effect. Its limits begin wherever that transaction stops.`)
],diagrams:[d('Local transactions surround effects; delivery crosses boundaries','flowchart LR\nC[Stable client key] --> T[Transaction: business state plus outbox]\nT -.->|at-least-once publish E| R[Relay]\nR -.->|possible duplicate E| I[Consumer transaction: inbox plus projection]\nI --> P[Derived state]','The repeated event can be safe because each consumer establishes its own atomic identity/effect boundary. External effects require receiver cooperation.',['Reuse one logical request identity.','Commit local change and outgoing intent together.','Allow retried publication with the same event identity.','Atomically deduplicate at the receiving effect boundary.'])],exercise:{prompt:'A tenant-scoped payment endpoint stores request keys but omits the amount from its fingerprint and deletes keys after one hour. A client retries with a changed amount after a timeout, then an old request retries the next day. Identify both contract defects and the external-provider boundary.',rubric:['Fingerprints all semantic fields.','Scopes identity and retry horizon.','Requires provider-side retry/reconciliation semantics.'],solution:'The changed amount must not be accepted as the same logical operation: include amount, currency, destination and other effect-defining fields in a versioned canonical fingerprint and return conflict on mismatch. One-hour retention cannot promise safe next-day retry unless a durable business operation identity still prevents a second effect; define and enforce the horizon. Local records also cannot atomically determine a provider charge after a lost response. Reuse the provider’s supported idempotency identity and reconcile uncertain outcomes rather than charging again under a new key.'},questions:[c('q1','A new idempotency key for every retry protects one logical operation:',['True','False'],'False','The receiver sees distinct operations when identities change.'),c('q2','Same scoped key with a changed amount should:',['Replay unrelated success silently','Conflict'],'Conflict','The key identifies one specific operation; semantic payload reuse must be detected.'),n('q3','An operation is retried4 times under the same key after its protected effect committed. Desired additional effects?',0,'effects','All retries return the prior outcome without a second protected mutation.'),o('q4','Why can an outbox publisher still duplicate delivery?','It can send successfully and crash before recording publication, so retry sends the same event again; receiving effects must tolerate that identity.')],flashcards:[['Idempotency key scope?','Tenant/caller plus operation family and logical request identity.'],['Fingerprint purpose?','Detect semantic payload changes under a reused identity.'],['Inbox?','Records applied incoming identities with the local effect.'],['Outbox?','Stores outgoing intent with the local business transaction.'],['Retention horizon?','How long a retry identity remains recognized; part of the API contract.']],mentalModel:'Repeated attempts are safe only when one durable identity and the protected effect share a reliable decision boundary.',sources:[aws,sql,rabbit]});

const overload=source('Google SRE book: handling overload','https://sre.google/sre-book/handling-overload/');
const breaker=source('Microsoft Azure Architecture Center: Circuit Breaker pattern','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/circuit-breaker');
const bulkhead=source('Microsoft Azure Architecture Center: Bulkhead pattern','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/bulkhead');

writeLesson('094',{objectives:['Allocate attempts and waiting within an end-to-end deadline.','Compute retry amplification across service layers.','Use capped backoff, jitter and a retry budget without retrying unsafe operations.'],retrieval:['L027: distinguish timeout from known failure.','L093: which identity must a retry preserve?'],sections:[
s('deadline','The caller’s deadline is a finite resource',`${pacing} A browser allows1 second for a result. Its API calls a catalog service, which calls a database. If each layer independently uses a1-second timeout and three retries, the user’s deadline can expire while several layers continue expensive work. Passing a remaining deadline gives downstream calls a shared budget rather than starting a new full timer at every hop.

Budget connection establishment, request transmission, server work and response handling according to the client library’s actual timeout semantics. Some APIs distinguish connect/read deadlines; others include different stages. Do not assume one option covers DNS, TLS and every read without checking its contract. Reserve a small amount of time to return a useful error or fallback instead of spending the entire user deadline on the last attempt.

Cancel work that is no longer useful when the dependency supports cancellation, while remembering that cancellation does not necessarily undo a committed effect. A business operation may still require reconciliation after the caller stops waiting.`),
s('amplification','Retries multiply across ownership boundaries',`Suppose three nested layers each permit up to four attempts, including the original. In the worst case one user request can trigger4×4×4=64 attempts at the deepest dependency. Even if many attempts fail quickly, this traffic arrives precisely when the dependency may already be unhealthy. Four retries plus the original would mean five attempts, so state your counting convention explicitly.

Prefer one clearly owned retry boundary for a dependency call where practical, and propagate evidence so outer layers do not blindly multiply retries. A retry budget limits additional work relative to original traffic or a fixed resource allowance. A token bucket can cap retry traffic independently of new requests. This is a load policy, separate from idempotency: a perfectly idempotent request can still overload a server when retried too aggressively.

Classify outcomes. Transient connection failures or explicit retryable overload responses may justify retry if the operation is safe and enough deadline remains. Validation failures, authorization denials and permanent not-found conditions generally need a changed request rather than repetition. Follow a provider’s documented retry guidance; do not universally retry every non-success status.`),
s('schedule','Backoff with jitter spreads attempts, then the deadline still wins',`A capped exponential schedule can choose an upper bound min(cap, base×2^retry_index). Full jitter samples a delay between zero and that bound. Different clients then tend not to retry at exactly the same times after a synchronized outage. Randomness helps distribution; it does not make unlimited retries safe or guarantee every group of clients separates perfectly.

For a worked budget, allow four attempts of200 ms each and delays100,200,400 ms before the later attempts. The worst-case total is800+700=1,500 ms before other overhead, so it cannot fit a1,000 ms deadline. Reduce attempts, shorten only justified per-attempt limits, or stop early when insufficient time remains. A jittered average below the deadline is not enough if the client promises a hard upper bound.

This executable Python arithmetic checks the worst-case schedule; it does not call a dependency:

~~~python
attempts_ms = [200, 200, 200, 200]
backoff_caps_ms = [100, 200, 400]
assert sum(attempts_ms) + sum(backoff_caps_ms) == 1500
~~~

Before sleeping, compare the remaining deadline with the proposed delay and minimum useful next-attempt budget. A retry that cannot finish in time may consume capacity without helping the user.`),
s('observe','Recovery traffic needs its own evidence',`Track original request rate, attempt rate, retries per logical request, deadline-exhausted failures and success-after-retry. A rising success rate can hide excessive attempt volume if every request now takes three tries. Correlate retries with backend saturation and client-side queues. Respect overload retry-after hints when they fit the overall policy, but avoid trusting arbitrary unbounded delay from an unvalidated source.

Load-test a failure and recovery transition. A synchronized cohort can overwhelm a newly healthy backend if all clients reset together; retain jitter and admission controls through recovery. Avoid retrying a partial user-visible stream as if it were a fresh invisible request, because duplicate output may break the user contract.

The useful interview defense combines semantic safety, bounded attempt count, bounded aggregate load and a deadline. “Exponential backoff” alone says only how waits might grow.`)
],diagrams:[d('One deadline bounds attempts and waiting','flowchart LR\nD[Remaining deadline] --> B{Enough budget?}\nB -->|yes| A[Bounded attempt]\nA -->|success| S[Return]\nA -->|retryable and safe| J[Capped jittered wait]\nJ --> B\nB -->|no| F[Stop and report uncertainty]','A retry is conditional on both semantic safety and remaining useful time. Retry traffic also needs an aggregate load budget.',['Start with the user deadline.','Make one bounded attempt.','Classify the failure and preserve identity.','Wait only if another attempt can still be useful.'])],exercise:{prompt:'A1,000 ms deadline allows three250 ms attempts with backoff caps100 and200 ms. Determine whether the worst-case plan fits. Then calculate deepest attempts when two layers each make three attempts, and propose a bounded policy.',rubric:['Adds attempt and wait time.','Distinguishes attempts from retries.','Controls one retry boundary and aggregate load.'],solution:'The planned bound is3×250+100+200=1,050 ms before connection/serialization overhead, so it does not fit1,000 ms. Stop before the third attempt when the remaining budget is insufficient, or select a smaller justified plan such as two attempts with one bounded wait. Two independent three-attempt layers can cause3×3=9 deepest attempts. Assign retry ownership at one boundary where possible, preserve the logical idempotency key, classify permanent versus transient outcomes, and cap aggregate retry traffic so recovery does not create a storm.'},questions:[n('q1','Three nested layers with four attempts each can produce how many deepest attempts?',64,'attempts','4×4×4 =64; this counts originals within each four.'),c('q2','Idempotency alone makes unlimited retries operationally safe:',['True','False'],'False','Repeated safe effects can still consume enough resources to cause overload.'),c('q3','Full jitter is intended primarily to:',['Guarantee no two retries ever overlap','Spread synchronized retry timing'],'Spread synchronized retry timing','Random timing reduces synchronization without guaranteeing separation.'),o('q4','Why propagate an end-to-end deadline?','It prevents each nested call from spending an independent full timeout after the user’s result is already useless.')],flashcards:[['Attempt versus retry?','Attempts include the original; retries are additional attempts.'],['Amplification?','Nested retry counts can multiply at the deepest dependency.'],['Full jitter?','Choose a random delay from zero to a capped backoff bound.'],['Retry budget?','Limits additional attempts as a resource/load policy.'],['Deadline exhaustion?','Stop when no useful attempt can fit; an earlier effect may remain uncertain.']],mentalModel:'Spend retries from a semantic, time and load budget, not from optimism.',sources:[aws,rabbit,overload]});

writeLesson('096',{objectives:['Calculate backlog growth, buffer memory and approximate waiting time.','Place admission control before scarce resources are exhausted.','Separate global bounds from per-tenant fairness.'],retrieval:['L007: interpret Little’s law only under appropriate stability assumptions.','L094: how can retries increase offered demand during an outage?'],sections:[
s('buffer','Unbounded waiting is a deferred failure',`${pacing} A document-render service can finish80 jobs/s while a burst arrives at120/s. Waiting work accumulates at40/s. If each queued job retains32 KiB of metadata and buffers,2,000 queued jobs occupy about62.5 MiB before container, index and runtime overhead. More importantly, a job at the back of that queue may wait roughly25 seconds at80 completions/s under a simplified steady-service model.

If the user’s useful deadline is3 seconds, accepting a25-second queue is misleading even if memory remains available. Choose admission from the service target and workload, not from the amount of RAM left after a load test. A bounded buffer makes overload explicit; it does not increase service capacity.

Backpressure communicates limited downstream capacity to an upstream producer so the producer slows or stops. It works only when the upstream can honor that signal. Public clients, scheduled retry storms or an external event source may not slow sufficiently. Admission control and load shedding remain necessary at the authority that owns the scarce resource.`),
s('where','Bound every place work can accumulate',`Look beyond the named message queue. HTTP accept queues, thread pools, async task lists, database connection waiters, broker prefetch and response buffers can all hold work. A service with a bounded broker queue but unbounded in-memory executor submission is still vulnerable to overload. Define maximum in-flight work and pending work at each relevant boundary.

Reject or defer before spending the expensive resource. For example, acquire a render slot before downloading a giant input and allocating its decoded image. Validate cheap syntax and authorization first where required, but avoid performing most of the expensive operation merely to discover that capacity is exhausted. A synchronous caller can receive an explicit retryable capacity response; an asynchronous workflow may receive accepted only if durable bounded backlog admission succeeded.

Deadline-aware queues can discard or cancel work that can no longer produce a useful result, subject to the operation’s side-effect semantics. Dropping an unstarted preview is different from abandoning a partially committed payment. State whether an accepted job can expire and how the client observes that terminal state.`),
s('fairness','One FIFO can let a noisy tenant occupy everyone’s wait',`Suppose tenant A submits10,000 batch jobs while tenant B submits one interactive job. A single FIFO can preserve arrival order while violating the intended tenant experience. Separate admission quotas, per-tenant queues and a weighted scheduling policy can bound A’s resource share and give B a chance to run. Priority alone can starve low-priority work unless aging or reserved capacity is considered.

Fairness should be defined in relevant cost units. One request that renders a1-page PDF and one that renders a1,000-page PDF do not consume equal CPU or memory. Fixed per-request tokens are a useful first approximation only under stated workload assumptions; weighted costs, time slicing or separate workload classes may be necessary.

Worked bound: if80 jobs/s is the completion rate and the queue-wait budget is2 seconds,160 queued jobs is an illustrative upper bound from Q≈μW. Real tail-service variation and concurrent workers require measurement and additional headroom, so do not present160 as a universal safe setting. The key is tying queue size to a time target rather than choosing an arbitrary large integer.`),
s('failure','Overload changes the failure mode you should test',`Inject a slow dependency, not just more clients. Service time rises, in-flight occupancy grows, and resource pools can saturate even with unchanged arrival rate. Test that accepted work remains bounded and rejected work fails promptly, with useful metrics and no retry multiplication. Compare queue age and deadline misses with completed throughput; a high acceptance rate is not a success if most accepted work finishes too late.

Track per-tenant admission/denial, queue age percentiles, in-flight work, resource saturation and cancelled/expired jobs. Consider degraded responses for optional functionality, but keep authorization and correctness checks intact. A cached recommendation can be stale; an authorization decision may need a fail-safe policy.

The common misconception is “make the queue bigger to fix overload.” A larger queue can conceal the symptom for a while and amplify recovery time. Bound work, communicate capacity, and decide deliberately which work is worth completing.`)
],diagrams:[d('Admission and scheduling protect a bounded worker pool','flowchart LR\nA[Tenant A] --> Q[Per-tenant admission]\nB[Tenant B] --> Q\nQ -->|accepted within bounds| S[Fair bounded queues]\nQ -->|over limit| R[Explicit reject or defer]\nS --> W[Bounded worker slots]\nW --> D[Dependency with deadline]','Limits exist both before queues and at execution slots. Fair scheduling is a separate policy from a global memory bound.',['Validate and identify workload scope.','Admit only within useful capacity limits.','Schedule across tenants without unlimited hoarding.','Execute with bounded downstream resources.'])],exercise:{prompt:'A service finishes100 jobs/s. Each queued job uses16 KiB and your approximate wait budget is1.5 seconds. Estimate a queue bound and its metadata footprint, then name two reasons the estimate needs validation.',rubric:['Uses Q≈μW with assumptions.','Computes consistent memory units.','Recognizes heavy tails and hidden queues.'],solution:'The illustrative bound is100×1.5=150 queued jobs. At16KiB each, that is2,400KiB or about2.344MiB of retained payload/metadata before runtime overhead. This approximation assumes a stable effective completion rate and relatively regular work; heavy-tailed service times or a slow dependency can raise waits. Hidden connection/task queues can add waiting outside the counted queue. Load/fault tests must validate tail latency and total in-flight memory, and per-tenant fairness remains a separate policy.'},questions:[n('q1','Arrival120/s minus completion80/s yields backlog growth?',40,'jobs/s','The excess offered rate is40/s.'),c('q2','A bounded broker queue guarantees all application queues are bounded:',['Yes','No'],'No','Executors, sockets and connection waiters can accumulate work independently.'),c('q3','FIFO alone guarantees tenant fairness:',['True','False'],'False','A noisy tenant can occupy most of the queue; fairness needs a separate scheduling/admission contract.'),o('q4','Why reject before expensive resource acquisition?','It avoids consuming most of the scarce capacity on work that cannot be admitted or finish within its useful deadline.')],flashcards:[['Backpressure?','A signal that downstream capacity is limited and upstream should slow.'],['Admission control?','Decide which new work may consume bounded capacity.'],['Queue age versus depth?','Age exposes user-visible waiting even when item count is modest.'],['Approximate wait?','Queued work divided by completion rate under a simplified service model.'],['Fairness?','An explicit allocation policy, often per tenant and cost class.']],mentalModel:'A queue stores waiting obligations; bound those obligations by useful time and scarce resources.',sources:[overload]});

writeLesson('097',{objectives:['Trace closed, open and half-open breaker states.','Use bulkheads to contain resource exhaustion.','Defend safe fallback behavior and recovery probes.'],retrieval:['L094: why do aggressive retries worsen a dependency outage?','L096: which resources can saturate before CPU does?'],sections:[
s('contain','Do not let an optional dependency consume the whole request path',`${pacing} An online shop’s recommendation service slows from20 ms to5 seconds. Checkout does not logically require recommendations, yet both calls share one connection/thread pool. Recommendation calls occupy the pool, and checkout begins timing out before it can reach its own healthy payment dependency. The outage spreads through shared resources rather than through a direct data dependency.

A bulkhead partitions resource budgets: separate bounded pools or concurrency quotas for dependency classes, tenants or criticality levels. The goal is to limit how much one failing area can consume. Isolation costs some utilization because idle capacity in one partition may not automatically serve another, so choose boundaries from failure impact rather than drawing a separate process for every function.

A circuit breaker changes whether a caller attempts work against a dependency that appears unhealthy. It is not the same as a timeout, queue limit or rate limiter. Those mechanisms can cooperate: timeouts bound individual waits, bulkheads bound concurrent resource use, and a breaker reduces futile calls during a failure interval.`),
s('states','Define the state machine and its evidence',`In closed state, calls are permitted and outcomes update a rolling error/latency sample. When the configured failure condition is met, the breaker opens and promptly rejects or routes calls to a justified fallback. After a cooldown it enters half-open state, admitting a small controlled number of probes. Sufficient healthy probes close it; a failed probe reopens it.

Use a minimum sample size and an explicit error classification. One malformed user request should not necessarily trip a service-health breaker, while repeated connection timeouts may. A failure ratio without a denominator or observation window is incomplete. Distinguish backend overload signals, application validation errors and transport errors according to the dependency contract.

Half-open must remain bounded. If every caller interprets the cooldown as permission to send a full backlog, recovery recreates the outage. In a distributed client fleet, independent breakers can still synchronize; jitter, probe budgets and server admission limits can help. There is no universal threshold that fits every workload without measurement.`),
s('fallback','Degradation must preserve the important contract',`For optional recommendations, a fallback can omit the panel or return a stale validated cache. The checkout path can then remain available if its own dependencies are healthy. For authorization, a fallback that simply accepts every request changes the security contract and is usually unacceptable. For a balance mutation, returning cached success would fabricate a committed effect. Treat fallback as a product decision, not a catch-all exception handler.

Worked resource budget: a service allows100 concurrent outbound operations. Allocate at most20 to recommendations and reserve a separate40 for checkout-critical calls, leaving other budgets explicitly accounted for. If recommendations hang, their twenty occupied slots cannot consume checkout’s reserved pool. That does not guarantee checkout success: shared CPU, database connections or a common network bottleneck may still couple the paths.

Breaker events should be visible in metrics and user behavior. A fast fallback response may count as a degraded outcome even when HTTP returns200. Otherwise the service-level dashboard can appear healthy while the feature is absent for hours. Track open duration, probe outcomes, fallbacks and dependency successes separately.`),
s('test','Exercise the recovery transition, not only the outage',`A deterministic state-machine test uses an injected clock: permit calls until the chosen failure threshold, verify immediate rejection while open, advance to cooldown, admit only the allowed probes, and reopen or close according to outcomes. An integration test adds a deliberately slow dependency and checks that the protected pool remains bounded.

Also test a flapping dependency. Rapid open/close transitions can create unstable load and user experience; tune observation windows and recovery criteria from evidence. A permanently open breaker without alerting can hide a resolved backend and become its own outage. Provide diagnostics and an operational way to inspect policy without bypassing safety blindly.

Common misconceptions are that a breaker increases backend capacity, that bulkheads isolate every shared resource automatically, or that a fallback is correct because it is fast. The desired outcome is limited damage and a controlled return to normal behavior.`)
],diagrams:[d('Controlled probes decide when calls resume','stateDiagram-v2\n[*] --> closed\nclosed --> open: failure condition met\nopen --> half_open: cooldown elapsed\nhalf_open --> closed: bounded probes succeed\nhalf_open --> open: probe fails\nopen --> open: fail fast or valid fallback','A half-open state admits a limited test of recovery; it does not release an unbounded backlog.',['Measure classified outcomes while closed.','Open after the defined evidence threshold.','Wait without spending full dependency resources.','Use bounded probes to decide recovery.'])],exercise:{prompt:'Design a breaker and bulkhead policy for optional recommendations sharing a service with authorization. State which fallback is acceptable for each, what trips the breaker, and one recovery test.',rubric:['Separates resource pools or quotas.','Avoids fail-open authorization.','Defines bounded probes and measurable criteria.'],solution:'Give recommendations a bounded independent concurrency pool and a timeout; allow omission or a validated stale recommendation result when its breaker opens. Authorization needs a policy consistent with security, such as rejecting when fresh authorization is required and unavailable, not accepting everyone. Define a rolling minimum sample size and relevant transport/timeout failures for each breaker. Test with a fake clock that open calls do not occupy dependency slots, half-open admits only the configured probes, and failure returns to open while sustained healthy probes restore normal calls. Measure degraded responses separately from full-feature success.'},questions:[c('q1','A circuit breaker is primarily:',['A policy for avoiding repeated calls to a failing dependency','A guarantee of more backend capacity'],'A policy for avoiding repeated calls to a failing dependency','It changes admission based on observed health; it does not add service capacity.'),n('q2','With a separate20-slot recommendation pool, how many recommendation calls may occupy that pool?',20,'calls','The configured bulkhead caps concurrent resource use at20.'),c('q3','Half-open recovery should admit:',['A controlled number of probes','Every waiting request at once'],'A controlled number of probes','Unbounded recovery traffic can recreate overload.'),o('q4','Why can HTTP200 fallback still be a degraded outcome?','The response may omit a required feature or serve older data; measure the product outcome rather than status code alone.')],flashcards:[['Closed breaker?','Calls permitted; health evidence collected.'],['Open breaker?','Calls fail fast or use an explicitly valid fallback.'],['Half-open?','A bounded probe phase tests recovery.'],['Bulkhead?','A resource partition that limits one failure’s consumption.'],['Fallback correctness?','Must preserve the relevant product/security contract, not merely return quickly.']],mentalModel:'Contain resource damage, stop futile work, and restore traffic through measured probes.',sources:[breaker,bulkhead,overload]});

writeLesson('098',{objectives:['Specify durable job states and legal transitions.','Separate lease ownership from idempotent effect completion.','Design retry/dead-letter and operator recovery semantics.'],retrieval:['L087: what can a paused old owner still do?','L093: where does a dedup marker need to commit?'],sections:[
s('states','A job is a recoverable state machine',`${pacing} A thumbnail job can be queued, running, scheduled for retry, completed, cancelled or dead-lettered. These labels are useful only when transitions are defined durably. “Running” should identify an owner generation and lease deadline; “completed” should identify the result/effect boundary; “dead” should preserve a reason and an operator decision rather than deleting evidence.

Use a stable job ID and a validated payload fingerprint. An enqueue retry with the same identity/payload can return the existing job; a changed payload under that identity conflicts. Store enough timestamps to distinguish created, available, claimed and completed time. The worker’s local memory is not authoritative after a crash.

At claim, atomically select eligible work, establish a lease and new claim token, and increment the attempt count. Claim selection outside the transaction can let two workers believe they own the same pending row. SQL locking, compare-and-set or a broker’s supported claim semantics can implement the boundary; the choice must match the storage contract.`),
s('lease','Heartbeats renew permission, not certainty',`A worker can renew a lease while making progress. If it crashes or loses connectivity, the lease eventually expires so another worker can recover the job. A pause may outlast the lease, leaving an old worker able to resume. Completion must therefore validate the current token and deadline at the protected state/effect authority, not merely trust the worker’s earlier claim response.

Choose lease duration relative to observed task behavior and renewal failure tolerance. Very short leases cause duplicate work during transient pauses; very long leases delay recovery after a real crash. A heartbeat is not proof that meaningful progress is occurring: a stuck worker might continue renewing forever. Use separate progress monitoring and a maximum operation policy when needed.

The prepared P3 implementation uses a fake clock for boundary tests and fresh random claim tokens whose equality is checked in the queue’s transaction. These tokens distinguish claim generations at this one authority; they are not ordered fencing numbers for arbitrary external resources. The external effect still needs its own protection.`),
s('completion','Only one transition can establish the protected result',`For a local result ledger, complete(job_id,token,result) begins a transaction, verifies running/current/unexpired ownership, inserts the result keyed by job ID, marks done and commits. A duplicate or stale completion cannot overwrite a newer result. The following is a contract sketch rather than executable SQL for every database:

~~~python
with transaction():
    job = require_current_unexpired_claim(job_id, token)
    insert_unique_result(job_id, result)
    mark_done(job_id)
~~~

If the effect is outside this transaction, define idempotency at the receiver or an explicit reconciliation flow. A broker’s delivery acknowledgement should follow the chosen durable result boundary. Crashing after a local result commit but before broker ack can redeliver safely only when the repeated logical job recognizes the committed outcome.

Worked trace: A claims at t100 with expiry110. At t110 the lease is no longer valid under a strict greater-than deadline check. B reclaims and obtains a new token. A’s delayed complete must fail; B’s complete can succeed if its lease is current. The test should check the result as well as the job state, because a rejected status update after an already executed side effect is too late.`),
s('retry','Dead letters are a workflow, not a rubbish bin',`A retry policy classifies failure, records a bounded reason, schedules available_at after backoff and increments attempts according to the chosen definition. Transient network loss may deserve retry; an unsupported payload schema may require a producer fix. At the attempt or age limit, move to a dead-letter state that operators can inspect without automatically repeating an unsafe effect.

An operator retry should preserve the history and state what identity/effect policy applies. If the original effect may have occurred, reconcile before issuing a new business operation ID. Metrics should expose oldest queued age, running lease age, renewals, expired claims, attempts, dead letters and completion latency. Queue count alone does not reveal a poison job holding up one key.

Graceful shutdown stops new claims, lets current bounded work finish or safely relinquish its lease, and closes resources. If forced termination occurs, leases and durable state provide recovery. Recovery tests should include crash after claim, crash after effect, stale completion, duplicate enqueue, exhausted retries and restore from a consistent backup. L099 implements the first of these using the prepared skeleton.`)
],diagrams:[d('Job states preserve both work and recovery evidence','stateDiagram-v2\n[*] --> queued: durable enqueue\nqueued --> running: atomic claim with lease token\nrunning --> done: protected result committed\nrunning --> retry_wait: retryable failure\nretry_wait --> queued: available time reached\nrunning --> queued: expired lease recovery\nrunning --> dead: attempt or age limit\ndead --> [*]: operator decision recorded','A retry is a durable transition with a schedule and history. Expired ownership never authorizes the previous worker to commit a later result.',['Persist work before acceptance.','Claim with an identifiable lease generation.','Commit only under current ownership.','Retain failure and retry evidence for recovery.'])],exercise:{prompt:'A job takes about30 seconds, lease lasts10 seconds and there is no heartbeat. A pauses at second8, B reclaims at10, and A resumes at12. Define the necessary correctness check and two operational improvements without pretending overlap becomes impossible.',rubric:['Checks current token at effect boundary.','Addresses lease/renewal policy.','Separates duplicate work from duplicate effects.'],solution:'A’s old completion must be rejected by an atomic current-token/unexpired check at the protected result authority; a local check before its pause is insufficient. Use a justified lease duration and renewal mechanism that fit task timing, and monitor progress/maximum duration so a stuck worker does not renew forever. Duplicate computation can still occur around pauses and recovery. Protect any external effect with receiver idempotency or fencing as appropriate, and record stale-claim rejections so the lease policy can be tuned from evidence.'},questions:[c('q1','A heartbeat proves useful job progress:',['Always','Not necessarily'],'Not necessarily','A stuck worker can keep renewing; progress and ownership are different signals.'),n('q2','With lease expiry110 and current time110, is strict lease_until > now valid? Enter1 for yes,0 for no.',0,'boolean','110 >110 is false; the exact boundary is expired.'),c('q3','A dead-letter job should retain:',['Identity, failure reason and attempt history','No record after deletion'],'Identity, failure reason and attempt history','Recovery needs evidence and a safe replay decision.'),o('q4','Why are queue claim tokens insufficient for an arbitrary external API?','The external receiver must enforce its own idempotency or fencing; a local token check cannot atomically stop an already issued external effect.')],flashcards:[['Durable claim?','A state transition establishing owner generation, deadline and attempt evidence.'],['Lease expiry purpose?','Allow recovery when an owner stops renewing.'],['Current-token completion?','Rejects stale owners at the protected result boundary.'],['Dead letter?','A preserved failed-work record requiring diagnosis and an explicit replay decision.'],['Graceful worker shutdown?','Stop new claims and finish or safely relinquish bounded current work.']],mentalModel:'Persist work as a state machine whose transitions remain meaningful after the worker disappears.',sources:[rabbit,sql]});

